const englishLessons=[
{title:'Parts of speech',body:'Parts of speech describe the grammatical roles words perform in a sentence.',table:[['Noun','Names a person, place, thing, or idea','student, Naga, knowledge'],['Pronoun','Replaces a noun phrase','she, they, it'],['Verb','Expresses action or state','study, calculate, is'],['Adjective','Modifies a noun or pronoun','difficult, careful'],['Adverb','Modifies a verb, adjective, or adverb','quickly, very'],['Preposition','Expresses a relationship','in, under, between'],['Conjunction','Connects words or clauses','and, but, although'],['Interjection','Expresses a sudden reaction','Oh!']],example:'“The diligent student completed the examination.” Diligent is the adjective because it describes student.',mistake:'A word’s part of speech depends on its use. Fast is an adjective in “a fast runner” and an adverb in “runs fast.”'},
{title:'Subject–verb agreement',body:'A verb agrees with its subject in number and person. Words between the subject and verb do not change the subject’s number.',example:'Incorrect: “The list of topics are long.” The subject is list, which is singular. Correct: “The list of topics is long.”',mistake:'With either…or and neither…nor, the verb generally agrees with the nearer subject. “Neither the teacher nor the students are ready.”'},
{title:'Verb tenses and consistency',body:'Tense indicates when an action occurs. Use time markers and the sequence of events to choose the correct tense.',table:[['Simple present','She reviews daily.','Habits and general truths'],['Simple past','She reviewed yesterday.','Completed past actions'],['Simple future','She will review tomorrow.','Future actions'],['Present perfect','She has reviewed three chapters.','Past action connected to the present'],['Past perfect','She had reviewed before class began.','Action before another past event'],['Present progressive','She is reviewing now.','Ongoing action']],example:'“By the time class started, Ana had finished her notes.” Past perfect marks the action completed before another past event.',mistake:'Keep tenses consistent unless the time relationship changes.'},
{title:'Pronoun agreement, reference, and case',body:'Pronouns should refer clearly to their antecedents. Subject pronouns perform actions; object pronouns follow verbs and prepositions.',example:'“Between you and me, the second solution is simpler.” Between is a preposition, so use the object pronoun me. “Lara and I presented the report” uses I as part of the subject.',mistake:'Singular they is accepted in contemporary English when a person’s gender is unknown or when it is the person’s pronoun. Avoid ambiguous references such as “When Carlo spoke to Miguel, he was nervous.”'},
{title:'Sentence fragments, run-ons, and comma splices',body:'A fragment is an incomplete sentence. A run-on joins independent clauses incorrectly. A comma splice joins independent clauses using only a comma.',example:'Comma splice: “The passage was lengthy, I read it twice.” Correct: “The passage was lengthy, so I read it twice.” Or: “The passage was lengthy; I read it twice.”',mistake:'A semicolon can join two closely related independent clauses. A comma alone generally cannot.'},
{title:'Parallel structure',body:'Items in a series or paired construction should follow the same grammatical pattern.',example:'Incorrect: “The course requires reading, writing, and to analyse.” Correct: “The course requires reading, writing, and analysing.”',mistake:'Check every item in a list after conjunctions such as and, or, both…and, and not only…but also.'},
{title:'Modifiers',body:'Place a modifier close to the word it describes so the sentence does not imply an unintended meaning.',example:'Incorrect: “Walking to the examination room, the rain began.” Correct: “Walking to the examination room, the student noticed that it had begun to rain.”',mistake:'An introductory phrase usually modifies the subject of the main clause.'},
{title:'Active and passive voice',body:'In active voice, the subject performs the action. In passive voice, the subject receives the action.',example:'Active: “The researchers collected the data.” Passive: “The data were collected by the researchers.”',mistake:'Active voice is often more direct. Passive voice is useful when the action or recipient matters more than the performer.'},
{title:'Articles and determiners',body:'Use a before a consonant sound, an before a vowel sound, and the when the referent is identifiable or specific.',example:'A university; an hour; an examination; a useful lesson. The choice depends on the initial sound, not simply the first letter.',mistake:'University begins with a /y/ sound, so use a university.'},
{title:'Context clues and vocabulary',body:'Use definitions, synonyms, contrasts, examples, and cause-and-effect relationships around an unfamiliar word.',example:'“Although the instructions initially appeared obscure, the teacher’s examples made them clear.” Obscure means unclear; the contrast and “made them clear” provide the clue.',mistake:'Synonyms are not always interchangeable. Check grammar, tone, and the exact context.'},
{title:'Sentence completion and logical transitions',body:'First identify the grammatical form required. Then determine whether the relationship is contrast, cause/result, addition, example, or condition.',table:[['Contrast','although, however, nevertheless','The second method was shorter; nevertheless, it was accurate.'],['Cause/result','therefore, consequently, because','The sample was contaminated; therefore, the trial was repeated.'],['Addition','moreover, furthermore','The plan is affordable; moreover, it is practical.'],['Example','for instance, such as','Several skills, such as inference, require evidence.'],['Condition','if, unless, provided that','You may proceed if the instructions are clear.']],example:'“Although the first solution was lengthy, the second was more concise.” Although signals contrast.',mistake:'Do not choose a transition based only on how familiar it sounds. Check the logical relationship and punctuation.'},
{title:'Communication, clarity, and register',body:'Effective communication is clear, coherent, concise, logically organised, and appropriate to the audience and purpose.',example:'Wordy: “Due to the fact that the weather was unfavourable…” Concise: “Because the weather was unfavourable…”',mistake:'Formal writing should not become unnecessarily complicated. Use precise words and remove redundant wording.'}
];
